WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.609
Not every Hollywood release can be a home
run, but that doesn't make it any better when

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.890
you have to sit through a terrible movie.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.269
In 2018, these are the films that perked up
our ears but left us utterly disappointed

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:12.269
on release.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:15.170
These are the worst movies of the summer.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.320
Action Point

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.680
On paper, Action Point seemed like sure-fire
comedy gold.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.730
The film is based on the infamously, dangerously
real Action Park and features Johnny Knoxville

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.860
and his pals putting themselves in harm's
way for our entertainment.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.140
"Action Park, this place in New Jersey, it
is the most dangerous theme park of all time,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.140
hands down."

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.200
It seemed like it would be full of insane
stunts, but Action Point arrived in theaters

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.360
only to fall right on its face.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.470
Amazingly, many critics found Action Point
not insane enough.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.239
Reno News and Review critic Bob Grimm, who
survived the actual Action Park as a child,

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:49.239
called it

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:52.820
"basically an insult to the legend of Action
Park - or Death Park, as we liked to call

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.270
it."

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.270
Gotti

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:00.030
A passion project for star John Travolta,
the biopic Gotti arrived after cycling through

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.880
producers and directors at an alarming rate.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.860
Its release date was pushed back several times
as a result, and when it finally landed in

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.650
theaters, all that discord was visible right
there on the screen.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.110
Many critics praised Travolta's committed
performance, even though it was delivered

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.580
in the service of a borderline-incoherent
film.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.441
The New York Times Glenn Kenny concurred,
saying that

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:23.570
"That the long-gestating crime drama Gotti
is a dismal mess comes as no surprise.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:28.110
What does shock is just how multifaceted a
dismal mess it is."

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.100
The Happytime Murders

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.300
Starring Melissa McCarthy as a human police
detective in a world where people coexist

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.620
with sentient puppets, The Happytime Murders
had all the makings of a raunchy, edgy laugh-fest.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.380
Unfortunately the film arrived without any
of the laughs.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.260
The production is the first from Henson Alternative,
an adult-themed offshoot of the late Jim Henson's

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.640
studio, and was helmed by his son Brian - both
facts that seemed to depress the majority

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.640
of critics.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.130
Bloody Disgusting's Scott Weinberg summed
it up as

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.200
"ugly, mean-spirited and aggressively redundant"

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.990
and his fellow critics seemed to agree.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.100
Gringo

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.400
If this is your first time hearing of the
action/comedy flick Gringo, you're not alone.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.280
The film flopped spectacularly in wide release,
grossing only about $11 million worldwide

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.970
despite an insanely accomplished cast featuring
David Oyelowo, Charlize Theron, and Amanda

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:17.970
Seyfried.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.920
The story follows a mild-mannered American
who goes wild on a trip south of the border,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.750
but the film failed to make an impact on audiences
and critics alike.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.380
Rolling Stone's David Fear called it

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.730
"a 'romp' that's keen on going nowhere."

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:34.090
Most of the critical venom was reserved for
its unfunny, contrived mess of a screenplay,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.140
on which the film's amazing cast was apparently
completely wasted.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.630
"He has that, like, shoulder holster gun thing
under his arms!

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:47.210
Maybe he's a cop or like an FBI guy or, no,
a DEA guy."

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:48.600
Slender Man

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.930
Fans could be forgiven for approaching Slender
Man with a healthy amount of trepidation.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.690
Plot details had been kept tightly under wraps
during the film's production, and the entire

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.930
endeavor wasn't helped by the real-life tragic
case of a young girl was stabbed nearly to

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.930
death.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.540
But when the film hit theaters, it became
clear why we hadn't heard much about the plot

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.780
- there wasn't much of one to speak of.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.040
Critics absolutely flogged the picture for
its paper-thin story and lack of any genuine

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.850
scares, with John DeFore of The Hollywood
Reporter calling it

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:20.790
"One of the most boring wide-release horror
flicks of recent years" and it's hard to disagree.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:21.860
Kin

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.040
The debut feature of twin brothers Jonathan
and Josh Baker, Kin sports an interesting

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.100
sci-fi premise: a teenager and his recently
paroled older brother find a powerful futuristic

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.640
weapon while tangling with a local drug lord.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.680
Kin mashes up elements of road movies, family
adventures and futuristic thrillers, but that

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.970
wide net of influences is the problem.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.569
Critics almost universally complained about
the film's wildly uneven tone.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:49.310
The film also failed to hit the sweet spot
either as a sci-fi action movie or as a family

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.290
drama; as Jake Coyle of the Associated Press
put it,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.010
"Given the film's title, and that the filmmakers
are themselves twins, you would expect the

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.470
brother relationship at the heart of the movie
to be something more than it is."

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.730
Game Over, Man!

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:05.849
A sort of comedic spin on Die Hard, Game Over,
Man! features Workaholics stars Adam Devine,

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:11.540
Blake Anderson, and Anders Holm as regular
joes forced to fight back against terrorists.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:16.220
Unfortunately, the film is packed with unnecessary
raunchiness and vulgarity far more often than

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.579
that premise would seem to justify.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.759
[Gun shots]

00:04:21.759 --> 00:04:25.690
Charles Bramesco of The Guardian had one of
the harshest criticisms for the film, writing

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:26.690
that:

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.340
"With its inexplicable combination of phallic
revulsion, fascination, and revulsion at its

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.130
own fascination, this wretched film hates
itself more than we ever could."

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.240
The Darkest Minds

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.870
Director Jennifer Yuh Nelson looked set to
launch a lucrative new franchise with The

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.680
Darkest Minds, but the film wasn't good enough
for one movie, let alone sequels.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.560
By and large, critics found that Minds simply
wasn't original enough.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.000
Its extreme derivativeness wasn't helped by
a confusing script that rushed through important

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.710
plot points and even the photography was called
out for being drab and visually unappealing.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.370
Dana Schwartz of Entertainment Weekly called
The Darkest Minds

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:05.560
"a Mad-Libs version of a dystopian YA adaptation
done by someone who saw half of an X-Men movie

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.120
on TV once, with no depth, no new ideas, and
no point."

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.710
Life of the Party

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:15.320
Following a recently divorced middle-aged
woman that goes back to college, Life of the

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.460
Party seemed like the perfect setup for some
bravura Melissa McCarthy setpieces.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:23.940
But McCarthy and husband Ben Falcone, who
co-wrote the picture, inexplicably failed

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.050
to provide the comedy fuel to make this star
vehicle run.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:31.000
Critics lambasted the film and poor script,
with The Atlantic's Christopher Orr writing

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.000
that the film

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.840
"doesn't live up to either of the nouns in
its title."

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.380
"Oh, I'm sorry!

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.020
Right in the lady business."

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.240
"It's not funny!

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.260
It's not funny."

